                <pb facs="0257" n="221" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v29p2/pg221"/>

                <head>101. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>Although the United States and Japanese Government officials focused their
                    attention on the reversion question throughout the second half of 1967, both
                    sides continued to work toward reaching an agreement on the entry and berthing
                    in Japanese ports of nuclear-powered surface ships (NPSS). After the details of the NPSS visits were resolved, the Japanese accepted an aide-mémoire,
                    and the exchange of notes on November 2 set the stage for the arrival and
                    mooring of nuclear-powered warships in specified ports in Japan. The first was
                    the USS Enterprise, which arrived on January 19, 1968, at Sasebo. Documents
                    pertaining to the negotiations and finalization of the agreement, as well <pb
                        facs="0258" n="222" xml:id="pg222"/> as copies of the aide-mémoire, United
                    States Embassy note, and Japanese Foreign Ministry note are in the National
                    Archives and Records Administration, RG 59, Central Files 1967–69, DEF 7
                    JAPAN–US.</p>
            </div>
